ce."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_1","anchor_paragraph_p0033"],"type":"claim"},"claim_10":{"content":{"canonical":"(d) graph classification on MUTAG: GCN 83.54 ± 0.40 accuracy with 4.8K parameters vs QGCN 88.61 ± 0.66 accuracy with 4.4K parameters."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_10","anchor_paragraph_p0069"],"type":"observation_claim"},"claim_11":{"content":{"canonical":"Across all tasks and datasets evaluated, quaternion variants of standard GNN architectures frequently perform within a small margin of the corresponding real models and sometimes exceed their performance while consistently having approximately one-fourth the trainable parameter count of the corresponding real models."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_11","anchor_paragraph_p0064"],"type":"claim"},"claim_12":{"content":{"canonical":"Winning graph-lottery tickets exist for both real-valued GNNs and QMPNNs."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_12","anchor_paragraph_p0017"],"type":"claim"},"claim_13":{"content":{"canonical":"Magnitude-based iterative pruning identifies subnetworks that match baseline performance at sparsity levels down to approximately 1–20% of the original parameter count across multiple architectures and tasks."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_13","anchor_paragraph_p0072"],"type":"claim"},"claim_14":{"content":{"canonical":"QMPNN variants scale to large graph datasets and maintain performance comparable to real-valued GNNs while using fewer parameters."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_14","anchor_paragraph_p0073"],"type":"claim"},"claim_15":{"content":{"canonical":"on OGBN-Arxiv node classification, GCN achieves 72.8 ± 1.5 accuracy with 145.1K parameters versus QGCN 72.3 ± 1.6 with 43.5K parameters;"},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_15","anchor_paragraph_p0062"],"type":"observation_claim"},"claim_16":{"content":{"canonical":"on OGBG-COLLAB link prediction, QGCN achieves Hits@50 45.80 ± 1.35 with 6.4K parameters compared to GCN 44.02 ± 1.57 with 12.5K parameters."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_16","anchor_paragraph_p0065"],"type":"observation_claim"},"claim_17":{"content":{"canonical":"the quaternion reparameterization is effective on large-scale graph benchmarks."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_17","anchor_paragraph_p0032"],"type":"claim"},"claim_18":{"content":{"canonical":"When the three QMPNN variants QGCN, QGAT, and QSAGE of standard GNN architectures are trained under the same hyperparameters as those standard GNN architectures and evaluated on standard datasets, the quaternion models achieve comparable or better accuracy, ROC-AUC, or Hits@K (proportion of top-K predictions that are correct) while using roughly one-fourth of the trainable parameters of those standard GNN architectures."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_18","anchor_paragraph_p0051"],"type":"claim"},"claim_19":{"content":{"canonical":"Magnitude-based iterative pruning identifies subnetworks that match baseline performance at sparsity levels down to approximately 1–20% of the original parameter count across the architectures QGCN, QGAT, and QSAGE and tasks."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_19","anchor_paragraph_p0072"],"type":"claim"},"claim_2":{"content":{"canonical":"Representing layer weights as quaternion matrices and grouping four real feature channels into one quaternion reduces the number of independent trainable real parameters by a factor of four relative to an equivalent real-valued GNN weight matrix."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_2","anchor_paragraph_p0042"],"type":"claim"},"claim_3":{"content":{"canonical":"A quaternion weight mapping from $F$ real input features to $F'$ real outputs uses $F/4\\times F'/4$ quaternion entries, yielding one-fourth the degrees of freedom of the corresponding real weight matrix."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_3","anchor_paragraph_p0042"],"type":"claim"},"claim_4":{"content":{"canonical":"Quaternion matrix multiplications have the same asymptotic time complexity as the corresponding real matrix multiplications."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_4","anchor_paragraph_p0042"],"type":"claim"},"claim_5":{"content":{"canonical":"The parameter reduction described in claim 2 is achieved without increasing matrix-multiplication time complexity. [Referenced claim 2: Representing layer weights as quaternion matrices and grouping four real feature channels into one quaternion reduces the number of independent trainable real parameters by a factor of four relative to an equivalent real-valued GNN weight matrix.]"},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_5","anchor_claim_2","anchor_paragraph_p0042"],"type":"claim"},"claim_6":{"content":{"canonical":"When QMPNN variants of standard GNN architectures are trained under the same hyperparameters as those standard GNN architectures and evaluated on standard datasets, the quaternion models achieve comparable or better accuracy, ROC-AUC, or Hits@K (proportion of top-K predictions that are correct) while using roughly one-fourth of the trainable parameters of those standard GNN architectures."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_6","anchor_paragraph_p0071"],"type":"claim"},"claim_7":{"content":{"canonical":"(a) node classification on Cora: real GCN 85.2 ± 1.3 accuracy with 140.5K parameters vs QGCN 84.1 ± 1.5 accuracy with 42.1K parameters;"},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_7","anchor_paragraph_p0062"],"type":"observation_claim"},"claim_8":{"content":{"canonical":"(b) node classification on PubMed: GCN 79.1 ± 1.2 with 131.9K parameters vs QGCN 78.4 ± 1.4 with 41.7K parameters;"},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_8","anchor_paragraph_p0062"],"type":"observation_claim"},"claim_9":{"content":{"canonical":"(c) link prediction on CORA: GCN ROC-AUC 79.27 ± 0.47 with 96.0K parameters vs QGCN ROC-AUC 83.55 ± 0.03 with 27.2K parameters;"},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_9","anchor_paragraph_p0065"],"type":"observation_claim"},"claim_E07":{"content":{"canonical":"In Figure 2, performance on OGBN-ARXIV, OGBL-COLLAB, and OGBG-MOHLIV is comparable to the dashed pre-pruning baselines for the red/green-starred GLTs of GCN, GAT, and GraphSAGE after pruning to a weight fraction of 0.3, despite sparsity."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_paragraph_p0058"],"type":"claim"},"claim_E_imported_10":{"content":{"canonical":"On graph classification with MUTAG, QGCN yields accuracy 88.61 ± 0.66 using 4.4K parameters, whereas GCN yields accuracy 83.54 ± 0.40 using 4.8K parameters."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_10","anchor_paragraph_p0069"],"type":"claim"},"claim_E_imported_15":{"content":{"canonical":"QGCN accuracy on OGBN-Arxiv node classification is 72.3 ± 1.6 with 43.5K parameters, while GCN accuracy is 72.8 ± 1.5 with 145.1K parameters."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_15","anchor_paragraph_p0062"],"type":"claim"},"claim_E_imported_16":{"content":{"canonical":"For OGBG-COLLAB link prediction, QGCN's Hits@50 is 45.80 ± 1.35 with 6.4K parameters, compared to GCN's Hits@50 of 44.02 ± 1.57 with 12.5K parameters."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_16","anchor_paragraph_p0065"],"type":"claim"},"claim_E_imported_7":{"content":{"canonical":"In node classification on Cora, real GCN accuracy is 85.2 ± 1.3 with 140.5K parameters, while QGCN accuracy is 84.1 ± 1.5 with 42.1K parameters."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_7","anchor_paragraph_p0062"],"type":"claim"},"claim_E_imported_8":{"content":{"canonical":"On PubMed node classification, GCN's accuracy is 79.1 ± 1.2 with 131.9K parameters and QGCN's accuracy is 78.4 ± 1.4 with 41.7K parameters."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_8","anchor_paragraph_p0062"],"type":"claim"},"claim_E_imported_9":{"content":{"canonical":"For CORA link prediction, GCN ROC-AUC is 79.27 ± 0.47 with 96.0K parameters, while QGCN ROC-AUC is 83.55 ± 0.03 with 27.2K parameters."},"source_anchor_ids":["anchor_claim_9","anchor_paragraph_p0065"],"type":"claim"},"claim_O07":{"content":{"canonical":"In Figure 2, after pruning to a weight fraction of 0.3, the GLTs marked 